---

<Section id="work" title="Current work">
  <p>
    SSCCS is an entirely new computing model and a hardware architecture, so the research is
    ongoing. What runs today is the infrastructure stack, on the silicon and the computing models
    in use now, and each layer tests another part of the model against a working system.
    Work also runs in the other direction. Products, and the infrastructure elements that
    support them, are implemented and composed first, and what they require is what moves the model
    forward.
  </p>

  <dl class="defs">
    <dt>Field composition algebra</dt>
    <dd>
      A composed Field has to be reasoned about with the same guarantees as its parts, so the work
      runs through composition itself: a composite of two evolving Fields is versioned from its
      constituents and rebuilt only when an observation finds the version changed.
    </dd>

    <dt>Compiler pipeline</dt>
    <dd>
      The core compiler and runtime are under active development, with work on{' '}
      <a href="https://docs.ssccs.org/research/rust_baremetal.html">compiler pipeline hardening</a>{' '}
      and on <a href="https://docs.ssccs.org/research/riscv.html">hardware mapping</a>, including
      the decode path that replaces a hash unit with combinational logic.
    </dd>

    <dt>Domain instantiations</dt>
    <dd>
      The model is carried into the field by projects held by separate entities, which the foundation
      supplies with the open-source technology and works alongside. Commercial projects among them
      stand on their own, so the stack does not depend on a single source of support.
    </dd>

    <dt>Hardware validation</dt>
    <dd>
      Phased prototyping from software emulation to FPGA deployment, with a parallel track for
      radiation-tolerant platforms. Hardware answers the anchor set on the die, and the decoder
      is placed and routed as a physical block. See{' '}
      <a href="https://docs.ssccs.org/works/openhw_integration.html">hardware validation</a> and the{' '}
      <a href="https://docs.ssccs.org/projects/syntagma/hardware/">measured hardware figures</a>.
    </dd>

    <dt>Reference implementation</dt>
    <dd>
      The primitives run in code on four substrates that are built and checked by different means:
      a Rust reference, branchless RISC-V assembly, SystemVerilog, and an independent verifier. One
      anchor set pins the convention across all of them, so agreement is evidence about the model
      rather than about one implementation. See{' '}
      <a href="https://docs.ssccs.org/poc/">proof of concept</a>.
    </dd>

    <dt>Strategic partnerships</dt>
    <dd>
      Work with infrastructure leaders to extend the reach of agentic research environments, and
      contribution to established open-source projects, whose requirements come back into the
      research as constraints the model has to meet. There is a standing invitation to institutions
      that want to validate the model against their own workloads.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <p>
    The project publishes what it measures, including where a result is weaker than expected. The
    Tagma decoder closes the 12 MHz board clock at 16.79 MHz on an iCE40, and the series states
    plainly that this hardware is not a speedup: the software reference reads a coordinate faster
    than the gate-level path does. What the gates buy is determinism, energy, and a record layer
    addressed by coordinate rather than by index. The{' '}
    <a href="https://docs.ssccs.org/projects/syntagma/hardware/">measured figures</a> carry each
    number with the channel that produced it.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href={site.works}>Work notes</a> carry the measurements behind each of these as they are
    taken.
  </p>
</Section>
